{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-64515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=(n+1)(n+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-64515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ca407819104c03ed5984e2855cba1c8e1a298c60dde6ac1d4499675c6c7f08"} {"question" "q-64515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-64515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=(n+1)(2n+1)"] ["math" "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=(n+1)(n+3)"] ["math" "f(n)=n(n+1)"] ["math" "f(n)=n(n+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-64515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $18{y}^{″}-9y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{2(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ca407819104c03ed5984e2855cba1c8e1a298c60dde6ac1d4499675c6c7f08"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"] ["math" "f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=2n(n+1)"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $5{y}^{′}-2xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-2x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[5(n+1){a}_{n+1}-2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,5(n+1){a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ca407819104c03ed5984e2855cba1c8e1a298c60dde6ac1d4499675c6c7f08"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=10{a}_{n}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{2(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{5n+2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-101403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $xy+9{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }9(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }9(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+9{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n9{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[{a}_{n-1}+9(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,9(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ca407819104c03ed5984e2855cba1c8e1a298c60dde6ac1d4499675c6c7f08"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-9{a}_{n-1}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9n+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-102315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-365776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+2xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-365776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+2x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n-1}}{n+1},\\,n=1,2,3….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/state.json" "file_sha256" "020aec92777e5c8a5814831af7e5cca7877a98a2b5f1e4d35ee9174c21753f65"} {"question" "q-365776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ca407819104c03ed5984e2855cba1c8e1a298c60dde6ac1d4499675c6c7f08"} {"question" "q-365776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cou